On Fri, 16 Oct 2026 16:12:02 -0700, Pat Maddox wrote:
> Several major open source projects use a mailing list to distribute
> patches. I know some of them have different policies regarding how to
> post new versions - some post new versions as a reply to the previous
> version, whereas other post new threads and link to the previous
> version.
>
> What are the specific policies of each project, and why? Also want
> documentation and examples.

The projects fall into two camps, plus a few with no rule. Each entry
gives the policy, the governing text quoted verbatim, the rationale,
and one v2+ posting as an example. I fetched every document link
today and checked that it contains the quote. lore.kernel.org blocks
automated fetches, so the examples are patchwork mbox links; their
headers show whether the cover has an In-Reply-To.

New thread, linking back
------------------------

Linux kernel. Documentation/process/submitting-patches.rst, section
"Explicit In-Reply-To headers" [a]:

"for a multi-patch series, it is generally best to avoid using
In-Reply-To: to link to older versions of the series. This way
multiple versions of the patch don't become an unmanageable forest
of references in email clients. If a link is helpful, you can use
the https://lore.kernel.org/ redirector (e.g., in the cover email
text)"

The rationale given is that mail clients choke on deep reference
trees. b4 encodes this: send.html says it "edits the cover letter to
add changelog entries containing a pre-populated link to the
just-sent series" [b], which is where "Link to v1" comes from, and
its b4.send-same-thread option defaults to "no" [c]. Example: bpf-next
"bpf: Fix arena memory incoherence" v5, a new thread whose cover
lists v1 to v4 by lore link [d]. All six recent netdevbpf v2+ covers
the researcher sampled were new threads.

QEMU. docs/devel/submitting-a-patch.rst [e]:

"Send each new revision as a new top-level thread, rather than
burying it in-reply-to an earlier revision, as many reviewers are
not looking inside deep threads for new patches."

The rationale is visibility: a new version should land in the
reviewer's inbox as new work. Example: "[PATCH v3 0/2] io: make sure
we don't close stdio fds", no In-Reply-To [f].

GCC. contribute.html [g]:

"If you submit a new version of a patch series, then you should
start a new email thread (don't reply to the original patch
series). This avoids email threads becoming confused between
discussions of the first and subsequent revisions... It is a good
idea to send a final follow-up message to the original thread
indicating that a new version has been submitted."

So GCC adds a pointer in the old thread, which the kernel does not.
Practice drifts: "[PATCH v2 0/2] tree-optimization/115869..." replies
to v1 [h], though three other sampled covers were new threads.

Reply in the old thread
-----------------------

Git. Documentation/SubmittingPatches [i]:

"All subsequent versions of a patch series and other related
patches should be grouped into their own e-mail thread to help
readers find all parts of the series. To that end, send them as
replies to either an additional 'cover letter' message..."

MyFirstContribution says to pass --in-reply-to and "always use the
Message-ID from v1" [j], and builds v2 with format-patch -v2
--range-diff. git.git pins this in its .b4-config with
"send-same-thread = shallow" [k], overriding b4's default. The
rationale is that readers can find every version in one place.
Example: "[PATCH v2 0/4] object-file: fix '.keep' lockfile
ownership...", In-Reply-To the v1 cover [l]. In practice people
sometimes reply to the previous version instead of v1, so chains
still deepen.

U-Boot. doc/develop/sending_patches.rst [m]:

"Make sure that your mailer adds or keeps correct In-reply-to: and
References: headers, so threading of messages is working and
everybody can see that the new message refers to some older
posting of the same topic. Uncommented and un-threaded repostings
are extremely annoying and time-consuming..."

The rationale is that otherwise reviewers have to dig up the old
thread and compare by hand. Example: "[PATCH v2 0/5] tools: mkimage:
bound external data reads...", In-Reply-To v1 [n]. Practice is
mixed: three of five sampled were new threads, since b4 users get
b4's default.

DPDK. doc/guides/contributing/patches.rst [o]:

"If the patch relates to a previous email thread, add it to the
same thread using the Message ID: git send-email ...
--in-reply-to <1234-foo@bar.com>"

No rationale is stated beyond grouping. Example: "[PATCH v5 0/4]
wireshark extcap support", In-Reply-To an earlier version [p].

PostgreSQL. No written rule; it follows from the tooling. A
commitfest entry registers a mail thread, and cfbot tests the newest
attachments in "the most recently active thread" [q], so new versions
go in as replies in that thread. Submitting_a_Patch only asks for
"Message-Id-based links" to earlier discussion [r]. This is my
inference from the tooling, not a stated policy.

Either
------

Buildroot's manual [s] makes the reply optional and gives a tooling
reason: "The advantage of in-reply-to is that patchwork will
automatically mark the previous version of the patch as superseded."
Both sampled v2 covers were new threads anyway.

No list policy
--------------

Yocto/OpenEmbedded [t] requires format-patch -v2 and a changelog
under "---" but says nothing about threading. FreeBSD reviews in
Phabricator and updates the same review with "arc diff --update
DXXX" [u]. Debian keys follow-ups to the bug number [v]. Mesa moved
to GitLab merge requests and its list-era rule is gone. I found no
written rule for OpenBSD tech@ and could not verify LLVM's old
llvm-commits practice, so I left both out.

Why it splits this way
----------------------

1. Replying keeps every version in one place and lets patchwork mark
 old versions superseded. Git, U-Boot, DPDK, and PostgreSQL choose
 this. It works best shallow, always under the v1 cover.
2. A new thread surfaces the new version as new work and keeps the
 reference tree flat. The kernel, QEMU, and GCC choose this, and
 say so.
3. Archive links make new threads cheap. Once lore and b4 could link
 versions by Message-ID, the reply lost its main advantage, which
 is why b4 defaults to new threads.
4. Docs and practice drift in both directions (GCC, U-Boot,
 Buildroot). The projects that stay consistent pin the choice in
 tooling, as git.git does with .b4-config.

Where this lab stands: we already follow the kernel.
doc/mail-workflow.md says a revision is "a fresh thread subject
`[PATCH vN]`, with a `v(N-1): <message-id>` link in the cover", and
src/lab-process-mail/prompt.md requires the "Link to v(N-1):" line.
format-patch.sh refuses a reroll without --previous, which also makes
it attach an interdiff. The superseded tag in "[PATCH v3] notmuch:
Tag series a later version replaces" [1] reads those Link lines, so
we get patchwork-style supersession without the reply. That covers
the one advantage Buildroot cites for replying. Your later decision
to drop the Overcome-by reply, in "Re: [PATCH 0/3] notmuch: Tag
superseded series and hide them" [2], means we also skip GCC's
follow-up in the old thread.

I see no reason to change. If you want shallow reply threading like
git.git instead, it is a small change to format-patch.sh
(--in-reply-to=<v1 cover>) plus the doc and prompt; say so and I will
send it. If you want this survey kept in the tree, I can post it as
a zib note.

